<article class="env-lemma" id="0A05">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0A05">Lemma <span data-tag="0A05">15.93.8</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a ring and $I \subset A$ an ideal. If $A$ is derived complete (eg. $I$-adically complete) then any pseudo-coherent object of $D(A)$ is derived complete. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      (Lemma <a href="/tag/091R" data-tag="091R">15.93.3</a> explains the parenthetical statement of the lemma.) Let $K$ be a pseudo-coherent object of $D(A)$. By definition this means $K$ is represented by a bounded above complex $K^\bullet $ of finite free $A$-modules. Since $A$ is derived complete it follows that $H^ n(K)$ is derived complete for all $n$, by part (1) of Lemma <a href="/tag/091U" data-tag="091U">15.93.6</a>. This in turn implies that $K$ is derived complete by part (2) of the same lemma. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>